      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+10x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10-2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (10-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(x-5)}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+10x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+10x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=9} & ={(-\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+10x}})|}_{x=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-2(9)}{2\\sqrt{-(9{)}^{2}+10(9)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{2\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
